# Design System: Folio3 Parking

## 1. Visual Theme & Atmosphere
A **precise, civic, measured** instrument for staff and administrators: dark, layered, quietly technical, and built for fast scanning rather than spectacle. It feels futuristic through surface depth, exact rules, compact data and motion—not sci-fi decoration. Density **6/10**, variance **4/10**, motion **6/10**. Keep the chrome plain and pages free of oversized hero areas. Borrow **GitHub’s** discipline of reserving green for a primary action, **Workday’s** orderly employee worklists, and **ADP’s** sober treatment of records; depart from their light defaults with near-black green-tinted layers, tabular mono numerals and tighter, ruled layouts suited to desk-based review. Keep employee and administrator navigation visually distinct but governed by the same system.

## 2. Color Palette & Roles
One subdued green accent sits within the workforce/SaaS reference family; it is not a copy of a named product’s shade. Dark surfaces progress through three green-tinted steps. Use no gradients, glows or pure black.

- **Night Ledger** (`#0E1411`) — Primary page canvas; near-black with a restrained green cast.
- **Deep Work Surface** (`#121B16`) — Primary work surface for navigation and dense content regions.
- **Inset Bay** (`#17221C`) — Recessed surface for grouped controls, selected rows and input interiors; third depth step.
- **Instrument White** (`#E5ECE7`) — Primary text and high-priority values.
- **Quiet Sage** (`#A0ADA4`) — Secondary text, helper copy and metadata.
- **Rule Green-Gray** (`#35443C`, 55% opacity) — One-pixel structural dividers and table rules; use as a low-alpha hairline, not a shadow.
- **Measured Green** (`#3FA77A`) — The sole accent: primary actions, active navigation marker, availability state, selected control and visible focus ring. Use as a thin rule or small fill, never as a large luminous field.
- **Muted Brick** (`#C57970`) — Error and destructive state only; subdued semantic feedback, not a second brand accent.

Maintain WCAG AA contrast for text (4.5:1 for normal text). Status meaning must be conveyed by text as well as color.

## 3. Typography Rules
- **Display / page headings:** Hanken Grotesk, medium weight, compact and controlled; use size and weight for hierarchy rather than oversized type. Headlines track at −0.02em to −0.03em.
- **Body / controls:** IBM Plex Sans, regular or medium, 14px minimum for dense supporting text and relaxed line-height around 1.45. Keep explanatory copy to 65ch.
- **Mono / every number, code and time:** IBM Plex Mono with `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`; use for counts, timestamps, identifiers, tables and numeric values.
- Small uppercase labels use 0.08em tracking and restrained contrast. Use curly punctuation and en dashes for ranges. Use one typographic contrast—sans labels against mono data—not simultaneous width, weight and case tricks.
- Serif is not used in this software interface. Never substitute Inter or a generic system face.

## 4. Component Stylings
- **Navigation:** Plain, compact bars with text labels and hairline separators; active role and location are clear through a small green rule and text weight, not a glowing pill. Keep employee and administrator navigation consistent in treatment, distinct in structure. On narrow screens, collapse to a clean, touch-friendly menu.
- **Buttons:** Compact, square-shouldered controls with a 6px radius. Primary actions use a small green fill with legible dark text; secondary actions are transparent with a hairline outline. Hover changes the border or a small directional indicator; focus-visible gets a 2px accent outline with 2px offset. Press uses `scale(0.97)` for 90ms. No outer glow.
- **Tables and worklists:** Dense but legible, with compact row heights, tabular figures, clear column labels and 1px dividers. Use a narrow accent edge for the active or selected row instead of a colored card. On phones, reflow each record into a labeled vertical row group—never create horizontal page overflow or conceal essential information.
- **Cards and grouped surfaces:** Use only when a boundary clarifies hierarchy. Prefer the three surface steps and hairlines to elevation; no glass, blur or ornamental card grids. Maximum two elevation levels; default to none.
- **Inputs and forms:** Label above the field; optional helper text below; inline error directly below the affected control. Use a solid inset surface, clear border, and custom accent focus ring. No floating labels. Keep touch targets at least 44px on narrow viewports.
- **Status lozenges:** Small, restrained text-first labels on a tinted solid surface. Green denotes the relevant available/active state; brick is reserved for errors or destructive states. Never rely on color alone.
- **Loading:** Skeletons match the eventual row or field geometry, with a subtle short shimmer only while content is genuinely loading. Never use a generic circular spinner.
- **Empty and error states:** Use concise, actionable product language in the same typography and surface system. Explain what can be done next without decorative illustrations; errors stay inline and identify the affected control or record.
- **Icons:** Use sparingly, only where they speed recognition; one consistent 1.5px stroke and 16/20px scale. Labels carry meaning.

## 5. Layout Principles
- Use a declared 12-column grid on wide layouts, with content aligned to the same rails; cap the working area at 1440px. Favor a compact left-aligned work area with deliberate whitespace over centered promotional compositions.
- The visual language is data-first: prioritize ruled worklists, labeled controls and clearly grouped records. Do not use oversized cards, marketing hero banners, or three equal columns of feature cards.
- Use CSS Grid for layout, not percentage-based flex calculations. Every element occupies a clear spatial zone; do not overlap content or stack absolute-positioned text and imagery.
- Below 768px, collapse multi-column regions into a single column. At phone widths, preserve useful table information through labeled record groups, collapse navigation cleanly, and keep controls at least 44px tall. Support phone, tablet and desktop widths without horizontal scrolling.
- Keep body text at least 14px, allow headings to scale with `clamp()`, and reduce vertical section spacing proportionally with `clamp(3rem, 8vw, 6rem)` where section spacing is appropriate. For any full-height composition, use `min-height: 100dvh`.

## 6. Motion & Interaction
Motion is fast, exact and informative. Animate only `transform` and `opacity`; use rules and changes in state to communicate hierarchy. Do not delay access to content.

- **Motion tokens:** `--dur-instant: 90ms`; `--dur-quick: 160ms`; `--dur-base: 260ms`; `--dur-slow: 420ms`; `--dur-scene: 700ms` (within the 700–900ms scene range); `--ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`; `--ease-in: cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0)`; `--ease-inout: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)`; `--ease-spring: linear(0, 0.009, 0.035 2.1%, 0.141, 0.281 6.7%, 0.723 12.9%, 0.938 16.7%, 1.017, 1.077 21.4%, 1.121 24%, 1.149 27%, 1.159, 1.154 33%, 1.051 43.2%, 1.013 49.5%, 0.996 57%, 1.001 72%, 1)`; `--travel: 8px` (never exceed 16px).
- **Entrance:** On first load only, reveal page heading and first work region in reading order with a small 8px rise and opacity change, 60ms stagger, total under 700ms. Reveal later content in groups rather than animating every row. Content is present and readable if animation is unsupported.
- **Scroll-linked:** In utilization visualizations, draw the meaningful progress/measure line as the data region enters view; this represents capacity being read, not a generic fade applied to every section. Reveal once, then leave the state settled.
- **Interaction feedback:** Buttons depress to 0.97 scale; rows tint immediately on press; interactive arrows nudge 3px on hover where present. Inputs show the deliberate focus ring. All interactive controls define hover, focus-visible and active states.
- **State transitions:** Tabs and menus move their indicator or panel over 260ms. Accordions use `grid-template-rows: 0fr → 1fr`. Inserted rows use opacity plus an 8px shift over 160ms. Updated values use tabular figures and a 600ms, 6px digit-roll reveal. Use a shared View Transition for a record’s label and ruled edge when moving from its worklist representation into its detail representation; fall back to a short opacity transition.
- **Ambient (one maximum):** Only a status dot that represents a currently live availability state breathes subtly: `status-breathe 7.5s ease-in-out infinite`, with opacity moving from 0.94 to 1; no other perpetual loop on the screen.
- **Signature moment — Bay-mark handoff:** When a parking record moves from a worklist into its detail, its title and thin green row rule carry across together using `view-transition-name: parking-record` and a 420ms `--ease-inout` transition, preserving the feeling of a marked space being handed over.
- **Reduced motion:** Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, remove scroll-linked effects and ambient loops, make entrances immediate opacity changes, and keep state changes to at most a 120ms fade. Never hide content until an animation finishes.

```css
:root {
  --dur-instant: 90ms;
  --dur-quick: 160ms;
  --dur-base: 260ms;
  --dur-slow: 420ms;
  --dur-scene: 700ms;
  --ease-out: cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
  --ease-in: cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0);
  --ease-inout: cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1);
  --ease-spring: linear(0, 0.009, 0.035 2.1%, 0.141, 0.281 6.7%, 0.723 12.9%, 0.938 16.7%, 1.017, 1.077 21.4%, 1.121 24%, 1.149 27%, 1.159, 1.154 33%, 1.051 43.2%, 1.013 49.5%, 0.996 57%, 1.001 72%, 1);
  --travel: 8px;
}
@keyframes page-enter {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(var(--travel)); }
  to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
}
@keyframes measure-draw {
  from { transform: scaleX(0); }
  to { transform: scaleX(1); }
}
@keyframes value-roll {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(6px); }
  to { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0); }
}
@keyframes status-breathe {
  0%, 100% { opacity: .94; }
  50% { opacity: 1; }
}
.record-title, .record-rule { view-transition-name: parking-record; }
::view-transition-old(parking-record),
::view-transition-new(parking-record) {
  animation-duration: var(--dur-slow);
  animation-timing-function: var(--ease-inout);
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  *, *::before, *::after {
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
    animation-duration: 1ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 120ms !important;
  }
  .live-status { animation: none !important; }
}
```

## 7. Anti-Patterns (Banned)
- No emojis, pure black, neon, purple-blue gradients, glows, gradient blobs, aurora, scanlines, glitch effects or glass blur.
- No Inter, generic serif faces, oversized display type, excessive gradient text or unlabeled mono-only passages.
- No overlapping content, decorative absolute-position stacks, horizontal overflow, three-column equal card layouts, oversized hero regions or repeated card grids.
- No ornamental cards where a hairline or whitespace is clearer; no more than two shadow/elevation levels.
- No icons on every row or link, no sparkle/wand as a feature shorthand, no custom mouse cursor, no stock-photo collage.
- No fake metrics, placeholder copy, generic sample names, or AI copy clichés such as “Elevate,” “Seamless,” “Unleash” or “Next-Gen.”
- No “scroll to explore,” swipe prompts, scroll arrows, bouncing chevrons, pulsing CTAs or wiggling icons.
- No uniform fade-up animation on every section, far-off fly-ins, motion over 900ms outside a page entrance, parallax on text, scroll-jacking, or loops on controls that do not represent live state.
- No marketing-only defaults in app UI: pill announcement above a hero, logo strip, testimonial carousel, pricing tiers, repeated CTA banners, generic KPI card row, glass dashboard tiles, or mega-footer.

## 8. Voice & Font Rationale
- **Voice:** Precise, civic, measured.
- **Display:** Hanken Grotesk — its sturdy, compact forms feel considered and technical without slipping into sci-fi display lettering (Google Fonts fallback: Hanken Grotesk).
- **Body / Mono:** IBM Plex Sans / IBM Plex Mono — a clear, workmanlike pairing; Plex Mono gives every operational number and time consistent tabular rhythm.

## 9. Motion System
- **Tokens:** Durations and easing are defined in §6 and the CSS block: 90ms, 160ms, 260ms, 420ms, 700ms; `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)`, `cubic-bezier(0.7, 0, 0.84, 0)`, `cubic-bezier(0.65, 0, 0.35, 1)`, and the specified CSS `linear()` spring; reveal travel is 8px, maximum 16px.
- **Entrance:** Heading then first work region; 60ms stagger, under 700ms, with grouped later content.
- **Scroll-linked:** A utilization measure draws as the data region enters view, once.
- **Interaction feedback:** Buttons press to 0.97 scale; rows tint on touch; focus rings are explicit; row/arrow details change only on their own hover or active state.
- **State transitions:** Indicators move; accordions expand by grid rows; inserted rows shift 8px; updated numerals reveal in 600ms; shared record identity uses the Bay-mark handoff.
- **Ambient (max 1):** A genuinely live availability status dot breathes over 7.5s with opacity 0.94–1; otherwise no loop runs.
- **Signature moment:** Bay-mark handoff carries a record’s title and green rule from worklist to detail with a 420ms View Transition.
- **Reduced motion:** Disable scroll-linked and ambient motion, show entrance content immediately, and cap state fades at 120ms.

## 10. Component Substitutions
- Replace feature-card grids and bento tiles with the actual ruled worklists and grouped record surfaces the product needs.
- Replace dashboard KPI tiles with clearly labeled operational data and compact utilization measures; avoid invented headline metrics.
- Replace hero split layouts, repeated CTA sections, announcement pills, logo strips and marketing footers with plain role-appropriate navigation and work content.
- Replace status-by-color alone with compact, text-first status lozenges; green is reserved for the primary/available state and brick for errors.
- Replace glass, gradient and shadow-heavy surfaces with three solid tinted layers and low-alpha hairlines.
- Replace icons on every item with a restrained Lucide set used only for recognition; labels remain explicit.
- No pricing cards, testimonial carousel or social-proof block: these are not part of the requested app interface.

## 11. Human Fingerprint
- **Unexpected decision:** Treat the selected record’s accent as a painted bay-edge: one slim green rule, never a full green row. It makes selection feel specific to parking while keeping the dense work surface calm.
- **Radius family / elevation levels / grid:** Soft 6px radius family (nested radius = outer radius minus padding); two elevation levels maximum, with hairlines preferred over shadows; declared 12-column grid, 1440px content cap.
- **Image treatment / icon set:** No decorative imagery or stock photography; if an image is part of supplied product content, keep a consistent 4:3 crop in a thin ruled frame. Lucide icons, 1.5px stroke, 16px and 20px sizes, used selectively.
